    <Folder>
      <name>Intelligence Summary</name>
      <Placemark>
        <name>AI Council Assessment</name>
        <styleUrl>#intel</styleUrl>
        <description>The synthesized intelligence data reveals a globally distributed U.S. military posture characterized by sustained high-tempo operations across multiple theaters. In Europe, a robust airlift and refueling network is evident, with multiple B762, GL5T, and GA6C tankers (RCH600, RCH5093, GAF600, SPAR85, BAH16) operating at high altitudes to support long-range missions, while strategic airlifters such as C17s (RCH139, AHY6738) and a B748 (KAF001) conduct rapid deployment and personnel transport. ISR platforms like the A148 (26752) and A332 (SKY01) are active in the Baltic/North Sea and Middle East, respectively, reflecting persistent surveillance needs amid regional tensions. The Middle East remains a critical focus, with ongoing strikes against Iranian military targets and heightened maritime security concerns in the Strait of Hormuz, where transits remain low due to Houthi attacks in the Red Sea. In the Pacific, the USS Theodore Roosevelt (CVN-71) is leading a carrier strike group in RIMPAC 2026, demonstrating U.S. commitment to allied interoperability and deterrence. The USS Tripoli (LHA-7) and USS Essex (LHD-2) are deployed in the Indo-Pacific and Mediterranean, supporting amphibious ready group operations and multinational medical integration. These deployments, combined with training activities in the Mediterranean and European theaters, indicate a strategic emphasis on rapid response, deterrence, and allied coordination. The data suggests a U.S. military posture geared toward maintaining operational flexibility and presence in key regions to counter emerging threats and support allied operations.</description>
        <Point>
          <coordinates>0,0,0</coordinates>
        </Point>
      </Placemark>
